THE CORPUS IS NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY
------------------------------------
The experiment is run over ManConCorpus:

    A. Alamri and M. Stevenson (2016). A Corpus of Potentially Contradictory
    Research Claims from Cardiovascular Research Abstracts.
    Journal of Biomedical Semantics 7:36.
    doi:10.1186/s13326-016-0083-z
    https://staffwww.dcs.shef.ac.uk/people/M.Stevenson/resources/bio_contradictions/

It is licensed CC BY-NC-SA 2.0 UK by its authors. **No claim sentence, no
research question, and no other corpus text appears anywhere in this
repository.** `fetch_corpus.py` downloads it from the authors' own page and
verifies its SHA-256 before anything will run.

WHAT ./data ACTUALLY CONTAINS, AND WHY IT IS SAFE TO PUBLISH
-----------------------------------------------------------
`axes.jsonl` — for each claim: its PubMed id, a question id, the corpus's own
YS/NO assertion, and **a boolean per comparability axis saying whether the
sentence stated it**, plus a word count. The extracted axis STRINGS are not
published.

That exclusion is deliberate and was measured, not assumed. The extracted spans
reproduce a median of 59% of their source sentence, 157 of 258 records reproduce
more than half, and the worst case reproduces effectively all of it. That is a
substantial reproduction of a CC BY-NC-SA corpus, so it is not redistributed
here — regardless of how the licence might be argued. Booleans carry every
number reported in RESULTS.md. Anyone wanting the strings can regenerate them
locally in about fifteen minutes with `extract_axes.py`.

`baseline_*.jsonl` — for each judged pair: the two PubMed ids, a question hash,
the two assertions, the model's one-word verdict, and whether the pair was
checkable. These are measurements of model behaviour, not corpus content.

`questions.txt` — question ids and hashes only. No question text.

PubMed identifiers are facts and are not restricted.

TO THE BEST OF MY UNDERSTANDING
-------------------------------
This layout was chosen so that no licence interpretation is required: nothing
here is a reproduction of the corpus. I am not a lawyer. If you are one of the
corpus authors and disagree with any part of this, open an issue and I will
remove whatever you ask, immediately.

SHORT QUOTATIONS IN THE PROSE
-----------------------------
README.md and RESULTS.md quote a small number of extracted axis fragments — for
example 'patients with HCM' against 'HCM patients' — to show a specific failure
of the method. These are two to seven words each, no full claim sentence is
quoted anywhere, and they appear as criticism of the technique rather than as a
usable copy of the corpus. That is a deliberate distinction from the excluded
machine-readable annotations, which reproduced a median 59% of their source.
